# Compiler-special builtin type declarations.
# This file has no module body. It hosts canonical builtin type heads,
# Extractor result variants, Unit trait implementations, and hidden standby helpers.

@doc """
Standard `Unit` type declaration.
`Unit` is the type of `()`, typically used for effectful functions that do not
return a meaningful value.
"""
@builtin type Unit

@doc """
The result carrier returned by an Extractor.
`MatchResult::Ok(payload)` proceeds to the child patterns;
`MatchResult::Err(error)` reports failure with a concrete Error.
The canonical signature is `MatchResult<Value>`. A `defextractor` declaration may
write `MatchResult<Value, Error>` in its return position. An `ExtractorClosure`
type annotation uses `MatchResult<Value>` and omits the Error argument. This
carrier is restricted to Extractor signatures and return paths and cannot be
stored or called as an ordinary value.

Branch consumers discard the Error, while failure-aware binding preserves it.
Use a normal `Result`-returning function for a value-level API.
"""
@builtin
defenum MatchResult<$Value> {
  Ok($Value),
  Err(Error),
}

@doc """
A first-class Extractor with a function signature and lexical captures.
Create one with `*{|value: Int| MatchResult::Ok(value)}` and bind it to a name.
Its signature is `ExtractorClosure<(Int -> MatchResult<Int>)>`.
`defextractor` declarations may name the canonical `Error` in their
return type; an `ExtractorClosure` type annotation uses `MatchResult<Int>`.
The last input is the matched value; earlier inputs are pre-arguments.
Invoke it in a Pattern, such as `if_let(3, extract(value), value, 0)`.
Ordinary function calls and implicit conversions from normal closures are rejected.

extract = *{|value: Int| MatchResult::Ok(value)}
if_let(3, extract(value), value, 0)
# 3
"""
@builtin type ExtractorClosure<$Signature>

@doc """
Compiler-reserved callable category marker for REPL and doc surfaces.
`Closure` names the family of first-class callable values produced by function
literals and capture forms.
Its runtime-facing shape is still described by ordinary function signatures such
as `(Int -> Int)` or `(String, Int -> Result<Unit, Error>)`; `Closure` does not
replace those signatures.
Like the documentation-only `Tuple` notation, this name exists to reduce
confusion in help text, `:doc`, REPL inspection, and related explanation
surfaces.
Treat it as a special builtin type anchor for callable documentation rather than
as a separate nominal data type to model in user code.
"""
@builtin type Closure

@doc """
Compiler-reserved `match` clause-block marker.
Use level: intrinsic-signature-only marker.
`MatchArms<$Scrutinee, $Result>` does not describe an ordinary first-class
value.
It exists only to name the special clause block consumed by the `match`
surface.
Do not use it in user-defined parameters, return types, fields, bindings, impl
targets, or module/type declarations.
"""
@builtin type MatchArms<$Scrutinee, $Result>

