@doc """
Compiler-reserved delayed-evaluation marker for standard declarations.
Standard builtins and Trait methods can declare `Lazy<$T>` arguments. A user
impl inherits the argument contract of the corresponding standard Trait
method, including when it overrides a default method.
It is not an ordinary first-class wrapper type for user data. Independent
user functions and Traits cannot introduce Lazy arguments. Bindings, fields,
container elements and return types cannot store Lazy. Use an ordinary
`(-> T)` parameter when a user-defined function needs delayed work.

A direct call in a Lazy argument is delayed regardless of its return type;
a parenthesized expression is evaluated before branch selection. The compiler
normalizes the inputs to a common zero-argument callable and invokes the
selected branch once. A callable left in the result is returned without an
extra invocation.

A capture placeholder receives the normalized callable type, and `(&N)` means
the same as `&N`. Write a complete call such as `&and(&1, &2)`; a bare capture
of a standard Lazy special form such as `&and` is rejected.
A normalized `(-> Error)` callable follows the same storage and argument rules
as other callable values; Error remains opaque.

## REPL
xldr(1)> both = &and(&1, &2)
xldr(2)> both(True, {|| False})
False
"""
@builtin type Lazy<$T>

@doc """
Opaque ordinary value identifying a concrete deferror declaration.
A bare declaration name produces ErrorKind without evaluating its constructor;
an explicit call such as NoneError() produces Error. Grouping a bare name
keeps it an ErrorKind. Variables, parameters, returns, fields and containers
can carry kinds, including through generic functions and captures.

ErrorKind has no public constructor, fields, Eq/Show, or user trait impl.
Strings and Error instances cannot be converted into kinds. A kind cannot
construct an Error dynamically. Error::is_kind compares canonical identities
and returns Boolean without revealing concrete Payload fields.
inspect displays ErrorKind(canonical qualified name).

## REPL
xldr(1)> kind = NoneError
xldr(2)> Error::is_kind(NoneError(), kind)
True
xldr(3)> Result::recover_kind(Err(NoneError()), kind, {|_| Ok(1)})
Ok(1)
"""
@builtin type ErrorKind

@doc """
Compiler-reserved process initialization protocol marker.
`StandbyInit<$T>` may only appear as the successful value inside a Standby process
`@init` return type: `Result<StandbyInit<State>>`.
It is not an ordinary public API or data transport type.
"""
@builtin type StandbyInit<$T>

@doc """
Compiler-reserved ignored-input closure marker.
Use level: callable surface marker only.
`Hole` is not a first-class data type.
It exists only as the canonical builtin type behind `_` when a callable value
intentionally accepts an input that is not observed, or when both update-side
slots of `Facet<K, S, A, T, B>` are deferred. Do not use it as plain data,
field, container element, or return hole.
"""
@builtin type Hole

impl Eq for Unit {
  def eq(self: Self, rhs: Self) -> Boolean {
    True
  }
}

impl Show for Unit {
  @doc """
  Return the standard string rendering for `Unit`.
  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> to_string(())
  "()"
  """
  @builtin def to_string(self: Self) -> String
}

impl Convert<Unit> for Unit {
  @doc """
  Return the value unchanged when converting `Unit` to `Unit`.
  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> to::<Unit>(())
  ()
  """
  def to::<Unit>(self: Self) -> Unit {
    self
  }
}

impl Convert<String> for Unit {
  @doc """
  Convert `Unit` into its standard string representation.
  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> to::<String>(())
  "()"
  """
  def to::<String>(self: Self) -> String {
    to_string(self)
  }
}

@hidden
@builtin def Pending::<$T>() -> StandbyInit<$T>

@hidden
@builtin def PendingAfter::<$T>(duration: Duration) -> StandbyInit<$T>

@hidden
@builtin def Ready(value: $T) -> StandbyInit<$T>
